WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.954
A sperm whale can click at about

00:00:01.954 --> 00:00:05.734
236 decibels. It's the loudest animal

00:00:05.734 --> 00:00:08.194
on the planet. Sperm whales can hear

00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:10.434
each other in the ocean from hundreds

00:00:10.434 --> 00:00:13.074
even thousands of miles away. Some

00:00:13.074 --> 00:00:14.894
researchers believe that they're able to keep in

00:00:14.903 --> 00:00:16.974
contact with one another through these

00:00:16.974 --> 00:00:19.903
clicks on other sides of the planet.

00:00:19.903 --> 00:00:22.794
These clicks are so powerful in the

00:00:22.794 --> 00:00:25.254
water that they can blow out your

00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:27.264
eardrums easily. And they could actually

00:00:27.264 --> 00:00:31.374
vibrate a human body to death. So when

00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:33.264
you're diving with them it's a little

00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:37.284
sketchy. What I'm going to show you now

00:00:37.284 --> 00:00:40.974
is a video of some freedivers that I

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.654
ended up hanging out with. I wasn't with

00:00:42.654 --> 00:00:45.794
them during this expedition, but I was

00:00:45.794 --> 00:00:48.354
about six months later. We went on a trip

00:00:48.354 --> 00:00:50.003
together and I had the same experience.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:51.863
So clicks you're going to be

00:00:51.863 --> 00:00:54.744
hearing are not coming from a boat. They

00:00:54.744 --> 00:00:56.724
aren't coming from a camera. These are

00:00:56.724 --> 00:00:59.183
the whales clicking these guys literally

00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:01.614
from the inside out to see what they're

00:01:01.614 --> 00:01:04.704
all about. And the clicks were so

00:01:04.704 --> 00:01:07.493
powerful that one of these guys put out

00:01:07.493 --> 00:01:10.194
his hand to stop a whale from running

00:01:10.194 --> 00:01:13.044
into him. And his hand actually was

00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:14.294
paralyzed for about four hours

00:01:14.294 --> 00:01:17.094
afterwards. So he learned not to keep out

00:01:17.094 --> 00:01:18.864
his hand in front of him when he was

00:01:18.864 --> 00:01:21.504
diving. This is an emerging discipline of

00:01:21.504 --> 00:01:24.594
research and it's pretty sketchy.

00:01:24.594 --> 00:01:26.633
This is what it's like. I'm not going to

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.944
talk over this. We're going to play this

00:01:28.944 --> 00:01:30.984
kind of loud, so you can really get an

00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:34.694
experience of what this is all about.

00:02:21.704 --> 00:02:24.234
So you're going to notice the whale

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:26.424
is going to flip on its belly. That's where

00:02:26.424 --> 00:02:29.534
it collects those echoes, underneath its jaw.

00:02:29.534 --> 00:02:32.244
They don't use ears under water.

00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:34.874
We get a really good look at him right now.

00:02:49.154 --> 00:02:51.514
Okay, so i know what you're thinking:

00:02:51.514 --> 00:02:54.414
why the hell would anyone want to do that?

00:02:54.414 --> 00:02:57.254
I know that was pretty loud for all

00:02:57.264 --> 00:02:59.423
of us, but trust me in the water it's

00:02:59.423 --> 00:03:03.654
like four million times louder.

00:03:03.654 --> 00:03:05.004
Once you're hanging out with the whales for

00:03:05.004 --> 00:03:06.504
a long time--this didn't happen to me, but

00:03:06.504 --> 00:03:08.544
it happened to these guys, their bodies started

00:03:08.544 --> 00:03:11.034
heating up from being pelleted with

00:03:11.034 --> 00:03:15.384
all of that energy, after a while.

00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:17.814
Again this is pretty sketchy stuff.

00:03:17.814 --> 00:03:21.863
It's strange to do. But it's important to

00:03:21.863 --> 00:03:25.164
note that these guys are fascinated by whales.

00:03:25.164 --> 00:03:27.394
They don't necessarily want to

00:03:27.394 --> 00:03:30.974
freedive with them this closely. But they have to

00:03:30.974 --> 00:03:33.014
because it turns out the only

00:03:33.014 --> 00:03:36.054
way to really study these animals up close

00:03:36.054 --> 00:03:37.574
is by freediving with them. By

00:03:37.574 --> 00:03:40.094
approaching them in your natural form

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:42.254
and interacting with them in that way.

00:03:42.254 --> 00:03:44.654
SCUBA doesn't work, it's too loud.

00:03:44.654 --> 00:03:47.214
Submarines don't work; same thing. Robots don't work;

00:03:47.354 --> 00:03:49.154
they freak them out. But when you

00:03:49.154 --> 00:03:52.054
freedive with them, this amazing paradigm shift occurs.

00:03:52.054 --> 00:03:54.334
And they not only don't swim away,

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.964
but most of the time the whales will

00:03:56.964 --> 00:03:59.754
turn around and welcome you into the pod.

00:03:59.754 --> 00:04:01.884
Sometimes they orient themselves

00:04:01.884 --> 00:04:04.524
vertically and do this very weird sort

00:04:04.524 --> 00:04:06.714
of New Age thing around you.

00:04:06.714 --> 00:04:09.474
As they're clicking. It's extremely intense.

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.634
So the next question you're probably

00:04:11.634 --> 00:04:14.394
asking yourself is like why research

00:04:14.394 --> 00:04:16.554
sperm whales? Well it turns out that those

00:04:16.554 --> 00:04:19.494
clicks you heard they aren't just used

00:04:19.494 --> 00:04:22.164
for echolocation. Sperm whales and

00:04:22.164 --> 00:04:24.234
dolphins also use clicks for

00:04:24.234 --> 00:04:27.234
communication. And inside of those clicks

00:04:27.234 --> 00:04:29.214
is probably one of the most

00:04:29.214 --> 00:04:31.764
sophisticated forms of communication

00:04:31.764 --> 00:04:33.684
we've discovered on the planet. Could be

00:04:33.684 --> 00:04:36.434
more sophisticated than human language.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:39.774
Now I know that sounds completely nuts

00:04:39.774 --> 00:04:41.904
to all of you. And certainly sounded nuts

00:04:41.904 --> 00:04:43.404
to me when I first started researching

00:04:43.404 --> 00:04:45.474
this stuff a couple of years ago. But

00:04:45.474 --> 00:04:47.354
just consider a couple things.

00:04:47.354 --> 00:04:51.734
The sperm whale brain is about six times the size

00:04:51.744 --> 00:04:55.614
of ours. This is a picture of sperm whales

00:04:55.614 --> 00:04:57.774
facing us head-on. And that top thing

00:04:57.774 --> 00:04:59.214
is their nose and behind that is their

00:04:59.214 --> 00:05:02.154
brain. Now sperm whales also have

00:05:02.154 --> 00:05:05.334
a neocortex. In humans the neocortex

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:07.494
is believed to govern higher level

00:05:07.494 --> 00:05:10.054
functions like conscious thought,

00:05:10.054 --> 00:05:13.454
future planning, and language. Well sperm whales

00:05:13.464 --> 00:05:15.924
not only have a neocortex, but theirs

00:05:15.924 --> 00:05:19.534
is about six times the size of ours.

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:22.334
Sperm whales also have spindle cells.

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:24.474
These are long and highly developed

00:05:24.474 --> 00:05:26.354
brain structures that neurologists

00:05:26.364 --> 00:05:29.864
have associated with compassion, love,

00:05:29.864 --> 00:05:33.054
suffering, and... you guessed it.. speech.

00:05:33.054 --> 00:05:35.994
All of those things that make humans human.

00:05:35.994 --> 00:05:38.864
And separate us from great apes. Well

00:05:38.864 --> 00:05:41.934
sperm whales not only have spindle cells,

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:44.664
but have them in a far larger quantity than

00:05:44.664 --> 00:05:46.793
we do. And they've had them for 15

00:05:46.793 --> 00:06:03.294
million years longer than we have.

